/**
  @page guides-getting-started-first-problem Your first problem
  @brief Build, run, and inspect a complete Poisson solve.

  This guide turns the mesh-and-space setup from
  @ref guides-getting-started-first-steps "First steps" into a complete
  application. It uses the same executable as the
  @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson tutorial", which develops the weak form
  and explains the code step by step.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-getting-started-first-problem-math The problem being solved

  On the unit square @f$\Omega=(0,1)^2@f$, the scalar field satisfies
  @f[
    -\Delta u = 1 \quad\text{in }\Omega,
    \qquad u=0 \quad\text{on }\partial\Omega.
  @f]
  Continuous, piecewise linear finite elements approximate the field on a
  mesh of 256 vertices and 450 triangles. The boundary values are prescribed;
  the solve determines the interior field.

  @section guides-getting-started-first-problem-code The application source

  Save the following program as `poisson.cpp`. The mesh is scaled onto the
  unit square before the problem is assembled. The solver status is checked
  before the solution is exported.

  @snippet PDEs/Poisson.cpp poisson-source

  @section guides-getting-started-first-problem-build Build and run

  Save this `CMakeLists.txt` alongside `poisson.cpp`. The IO target is needed
  for XDMF and its HDF5 data files.

  @code{.cmake}
  cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.16)
  project(PoissonExample LANGUAGES CXX)

  find_package(Rodin REQUIRED)

  add_executable(poisson poisson.cpp)
  target_compile_features(poisson PRIVATE cxx_std_20)
  target_link_libraries(poisson PRIVATE
    Rodin::Geometry
    Rodin::Assembly
    Rodin::Variational
    Rodin::Solver
    Rodin::IO
  )
  @endcode

  Configure and build from the application directory:

  @code{.bash}
  cmake -S . -B build
  cmake --build build
  @endcode

  Run from a separate directory to keep generated data together:

  @code{.bash}
  mkdir -p results
  cd results
  ../build/poisson
  @endcode

  A successful run writes `Poisson.xdmf` and its referenced HDF5 files into
  `results`. A failed solver returns `EXIT_FAILURE` before export.

  @section guides-getting-started-first-problem-visualize Inspect the solution

  Open `Poisson.xdmf` in ParaView, select **Apply**, and color the mesh by **u**.
  The boundary values should be zero and the interior values positive. On this
  mesh the computed nodal maximum is approximately @f$0.072862@f$.

  The @ref examples-pdes-poisson-output "visualization section of the tutorial"
  shows the computed field and explains how to display it as a surface.
  The XDMF file describes the data; the HDF5 files contain it, so the files
  must remain together.

  @section guides-getting-started-first-problem-variations Modify one part of the problem

  Once the baseline solve runs, change one ingredient at a time. The weak form,
  boundary data, and solver must remain compatible.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-first-problem-non-homogeneous Prescribed nonzero values

  Replace the zero boundary data with a function @f$g(x,y)=x^2@f$:

  @code{.cpp}
  RealFunction g([](const Point& p) { return p.x() * p.x(); });
  poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v)) - Integral(f, v)
             + DirichletBC(u, g);
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-getting-started-first-problem-partial-bc Selected boundary regions

  `DirichletBC(u, Zero()).on(boundaryAttribute)` restricts the condition to
  boundary entities carrying that attribute. The mesh must already have the
  intended boundary attributes; choosing an integer does not mark the mesh.
  Boundary regions without prescribed values retain the natural condition
  implied by the weak form. See @ref guides-boundary-conditions.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-first-problem-variable-source A spatially varying source

  Replace the constant source with a coordinate-dependent function, for example
  @f$f(x,y)=xy@f$:

  @code{.cpp}
  RealFunction f([](const Point& p) { return p.x() * p.y(); });
  @endcode

  Rebuild the problem expression using this source before solving it.

  @subsection guides-getting-started-first-problem-different-solver Solver controls

  For this symmetric positive definite problem, CG exposes an iteration budget
  and a tolerance:

  @code{.cpp}
  CG solver(poisson);
  solver.setMaxIterations(1000).setTolerance(1e-10).solve();
  @endcode

  These values illustrate configuration, rather than a required setting for
  every mesh. Check `solver.success()` after the solve. A direct alternative is
  `SparseLU`; a standalone solver can also be passed to `poisson.solve(solver)`.
  The @ref guides-solvers "solver guide" explains the supported interfaces.

  @section guides-getting-started-first-problem-next Continue learning

  Read the @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson tutorial" for the derivation and
  the role of each form-language expression. Then continue with
  @ref guides-getting-started-core-concepts "Core concepts" or
  @ref examples-pdes-elasticity "linear elasticity" for a vector-valued problem.
*/
